WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:02.208
Remember when Pluto was a planet?

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.958
Until very recently,
Pluto's planetary status

00:00:06.042 --> 00:00:10.125
was an immutable fact learned by
primary school kids the world over.

00:00:10.208 --> 00:00:12.375
Pluto had an undisputed place

00:00:12.458 --> 00:00:15.083
in papier mâché models
of the solar system

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:17.167
and even its very own song lyric -

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.583
'And last of all there's
Pluto too...'

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:21.333
But in August 2006,

00:00:21.417 --> 00:00:25.042
Pluto found itself cruelly demoted
to a dwarf planet

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:27.667
after failing to meet
one of three criteria

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:32.375
the International Astronomical Union
uses to define a full-sized planet.

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:35.792
We are taught from an early age
to think of scientific facts

00:00:35.875 --> 00:00:38.000
as, well, facts.

00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:41.500
And it can be unsettling when this
turns out to not be the case.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:46.250
But does this uncertainty really mean
that we can't trust science?

00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:48.583
According to the Royal Society,

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:52.042
the world's oldest independent
scientific academy,

00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:54.375
the answer is quite the opposite.

00:00:54.708 --> 00:00:56.417
Questioning established facts

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.583
is actually at the heart of the
scientific method.

00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:03.250
Think of the whole process as a giant
system of checks and balances

00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:04.792
based on raw information

00:01:04.875 --> 00:01:08.125
drawn from experiments
or observations of nature.

00:01:08.208 --> 00:01:10.125
Those experiments and observations

00:01:10.208 --> 00:01:13.000
lead to the formulation
of a hypothesis

00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:15.875
which then goes through a rigorous
process of checks

00:01:15.958 --> 00:01:17.375
by other scientists.

00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:20.250
However, there isn't always
enough information

00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:22.208
to draw the right conclusions.

00:01:22.792 --> 00:01:25.042
Take for example Sir Francis Bacon,

00:01:25.125 --> 00:01:29.125
who noticed that the coastlines of
West Africa and eastern South America

00:01:29.208 --> 00:01:30.833
looked like they were symmetrical.

00:01:30.917 --> 00:01:33.625
He believed that nature
was copying herself.

00:01:33.875 --> 00:01:35.042
What he couldn't have known

00:01:35.125 --> 00:01:36.667
in the early 1620s

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.125
was that the two coasts are two sides

00:01:39.208 --> 00:01:42.542
of a fault-line in an
ancient supercontinent

00:01:42.625 --> 00:01:46.583
which split apart
140 million years ago.

00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:50.250
Bacon's hypothesis remained
gospel for centuries,

00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:53.458
until new discoveries about the
science of plate tectonics

00:01:53.542 --> 00:01:55.542
emerged in the 1950s

00:01:55.625 --> 00:01:59.292
and provided a neat explanation
for Bacon's observation.

00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:04.458
There's also the fact that different
experts examining the same raw data

00:02:04.542 --> 00:02:06.958
can draw different conclusions.

00:02:07.375 --> 00:02:11.083
Spare a thought for poor Robert Plot
who, in the 1670s,

00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:15.667
found a fossilised bone that he
thought was that of a giant human.

00:02:16.083 --> 00:02:18.792
At least he wasn't around when
a geologist and zoologist

00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:21.375
proved it was in fact a Megalosaurus,

00:02:21.458 --> 00:02:24.500
a kind of dinosaur that roamed
the Earth in the middle Jurassic.

00:02:25.167 --> 00:02:27.542
Sometimes the scientific
method is all about

00:02:27.625 --> 00:02:30.458
setting up ground-breaking
experiments at the right time,

00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:32.833
in the right place to test theories.

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:37.042
In 1919, a blockbuster meeting
of the Royal Society

00:02:37.125 --> 00:02:40.125
confirmed Einstein's theory
of general relativity

00:02:40.458 --> 00:02:44.333
after a solar eclipse provided
the perfect circumstances

00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:46.417
to measure the bending of starlight.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.875
This showed that the gravity

00:02:47.958 --> 00:02:50.333
of a massive object, such as the Sun,

00:02:50.417 --> 00:02:52.000
could bend light around it.

00:02:52.375 --> 00:02:55.292
Under the watchful eye of
Sir Isaac Newton's portrait,

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:58.792
the scientific community replaced
Newton's previous theory

00:02:58.875 --> 00:03:03.417
with Einstein's newer and more
general interpretation of gravity.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.792
But even with evidence in its favour,

00:03:05.875 --> 00:03:10.083
Einstein's theory still can't be
thought of as a fundamental fact.

00:03:10.167 --> 00:03:14.292
It's even possible that in the future
new breakthroughs will supersede

00:03:14.375 --> 00:03:17.042
our understanding
of general relativity.

00:03:18.458 --> 00:03:23.083
Uncertainty in science isn't enough
if all the perspectives are the same.

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:27.250
Sometimes a lack of diversity
in the system can skew the results.

00:03:27.458 --> 00:03:29.208
Until the 1970s,

00:03:29.292 --> 00:03:31.500
primatologists were mostly men

00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:34.417
and tended to limit their studies
to male primates.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.875
They believed that the aggression
observed in baboons

00:03:37.958 --> 00:03:41.750
meant human evolution had been driven
by similar behaviour.

00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:44.167
That was until anthropologist
Shirley Strum

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.125
began observing both
female and male baboons,

00:03:47.208 --> 00:03:49.167
disproving these earlier theories

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.125
and facing a backlash
for her troubles.

00:03:52.417 --> 00:03:55.250
Scientists don't always get it right
on the first go.

00:03:55.333 --> 00:03:58.625
But rather than instilling doubt,
it should inspire confidence

00:03:58.708 --> 00:04:02.833
that outdated ideas are replaced when
new information becomes available.

00:04:03.458 --> 00:04:06.042
It's the difference between
upgrading your mobile

00:04:06.125 --> 00:04:09.917
and clinging to your old rotary phone
because you don't want to be wrong.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.250
And in most cases,
newer breakthroughs

00:04:12.333 --> 00:04:15.625
would not be possible without the
legwork that came before them.

00:04:16.083 --> 00:04:19.625
While the rotary phone isn't the best
available technology today,

00:04:19.708 --> 00:04:22.083
your smart phone wouldn't exist
without it.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.333
Uncertainty is baked into
the scientific process.

00:04:26.417 --> 00:04:29.875
It's the fundamental reason
that progress is possible.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:33.125
Ultimately, it comes down
to who you trust more -

00:04:33.833 --> 00:04:35.958
the person who's certain
they're right,

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:39.333
or the person who's willing
to be proved wrong.

